From e81fd404a1895a78d64eb655435112412ba0e5f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 03:51:04 +0000
Subject: software_isp: debayer_egl: Bin the raw frame in 2x2 cells and filter
 them

The packed Bayer shader reads only the byte holding a pixel's 8 high bits
and ignores the 2 low bits, and an output smaller than the input takes one
pixel in each cell of the scaling, averaging nothing. In the dark, where the
signal is a few tens of counts above black and the tone curve then lifts it
by up to 16x, the 4-count steps and the unaveraged sensor noise are what
shows.

For frames where DebayerParams::denoise is enabled, and an output at most
two thirds of the input's size, work on 2x2 cells of the raw frame instead.
A first pass bins each cell at the full 10 bits into a texel of a 10-bit
unorm texture (bayer_cells_bin.frag: the red, the blue and the mean of the
greens, with two textureGather fetches), and the main pass, a second
program of bayer_1x_packed.frag (CELL_PATH) with the colour processing
shared by a function, samples the cells with the hardware's linear filtering
and filters them with a bilateral filter over five such samples, the pixel's
own and four at 1.5 cells on the diagonals. The filter's range kernel
follows the sensor's noise, a variance of slope * level + quad * level^2 +
floor with widths set by the tuning. It needs OpenGL ES 3.2 (textureGather
and the render targets) and falls back to the other path without it.

At 1920x1080 from 4080x3072 the two passes take about 25 ms of GPU time a
frame, against 13 ms for the single pixel path. On a synthetic scene with
the measured sensor noise, the error against the clean image is 1.7 counts
(10-bit) in flat areas at 16x gain, against 6.3 for the single pixel path,
and no more on sharp edges, thin lines and fine detail. On the handset the
output noise in a dim room is about a third of the single pixel path's.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
